":"the evolution of human sexuality","isbn_10":["0297817752"],"key":"/books/OL17398871M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL540031A","name":"Jared Diamond"}],"publish_places":["London"],"pagination":"viii,168p. :","title":"Why is sex fun?","dewey_decimal_class":["599.936"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes bibliography (p157-160) and index."},"identifiers":{"librarything":["50151"],"goodreads":["1450367"]},"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"subjects":["Human evolution.","Sex."],"publish_date":"1997","publish_country":"enk","by_statement":"Jared Diamond.","works":[{"key":"/works/OL3333181W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"source_records":["bwb:9780297817758"],"latest_revision":9,"revision":9,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-09-28T16:49:59.964391"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2025-06-12T17:19:22.352532"}}},"OLID:OL18385924M":{"bib_key":"OLID:OL18385924M","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL18385924M/Why_is_sex_fun","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL18385924M/Why_is_sex_fun","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/8734741-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["Basic Books"],"number_of_pages":165,"subtitle":"the evolution of human sexuality","isbn_10":["0465031269"],"series":["Science masters"],"covers":[8734741],"lc_classifications":["HQ21 .D48 1997b","HQ21.D48 1997"],"key":"/books/OL18385924M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL540031A","name":"Jared Diamond"}],"publish_places":["New York, NY"],"subjects":["Sex","Sex customs"],"pagination":"x, 165 p. :","classifications":{},"source_records":["marc:marc_oregon_summit_records/catalog_files/pcc_bibs.mrc_rev.mrc:118119366:1326","marc:marc_ithaca_college/ic_marc.mrc:171848159:1282","ia:whyissexfunevolu00diam_261","marc:marc_openlibraries_sanfranciscopubliclibrary/sfpl_chq_2018_12_24_run03.mrc:313458704:2344","bwb:9780465031269","idb:9780465031269","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_014.bib.mrc:183899331:3367"],"title":"Why is sex fun?","dewey_decimal_class":["306.7"],"notes":"Includes bibliographical references (p. 147-149) and index.","identifiers":{"goodreads":["1991"],"librarything":["50151"]},"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"local_id":["urn:sfpl:31223123558398"],"publish_date":"1997","publish_country":"nyu","by_statement":"Jared Diamond.","oclc_numbers":["40512300"],"works":[{"key":"/works/OL3333181W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"To humans, the sex lives of many animals seem weird. In fact, by comparison with other animals, we are the ones with the weird sex lives. How did that come to be? We are the only social species to insist on carrying out sex privately. Stranger yet, we have sex at any time, even when the female can't be fertilized (for example, because she is already pregnant, post-menopausal, or between fertile cycles). A human female doesn't know her precise time of fertility and certainly doesn't advertise it to human males by the striking color changes, smells, and sounds used by other female mammals. Why do we differ so radically in these and other important aspects of our sexuality from our closest ancestor, the apes? Why does the human female, virtually alone among mammals, go through menopause? Why does the human male stand out as one of the few mammals to stay (often or usually) with the female he impregnates, to help raise the children that he sired? Why is the human penis so unnecessarily large? There is no one better qualified than Jared Diamond--renowned expert in the fields of physiology and evolutionary biology--to explain the evolutionary forces that operated on our ancestors to make us sexually different. With wit and a wealth of fascinating examples, he explains how our sexuality has been as crucial as our large brains and upright posture in our rise to human status.--From publisher description."},"latest_revision":19,"revision":19,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-10-16T12:03:22.208049"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2025-08-19T01:56:00.969797"}}},"OLID:OL26967338M":{"bib_key":"OLID:OL26967338M","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL26967338M/Why_is_sex_fun","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL26967338M/Why_is_sex_fun","details":{"subtitle":"the evolution of human sexuality","description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"To us humans the sex lives of many animals seem weird. In fact, by comparison with all the other animals, we are the ones with the weird sex lives. How did that come to be? Just count our bizarre ways. We are the only social species to insist on carrying out sex privately. Stranger yet, we have sex at any time, even when the female can't be fertilized (for example, because she is already pregnant, post-menopausal, or between fertile cycles). A human female doesn't know her precise time of fertility and certainly doesn't advertise it to human males by the striking color changes, smells, and sounds used by other female mammals. Why do we differ so radically in these and other important aspects of our sexuality from our closest ancestor, the apes? Why does the human female, virtually alone among mammals go through menopause? Why does the human male stand out as one of the few mammals to stay (often or usually) with the female he impregnates, to help raise the children that he sired? Why is the human penis so unnecessarily large? There is no one better qualified than Jared Diamond-renowned expert in the fields of physiology and evolutionary biology and award-winning author-to explain the evolutionary forces that operated on our ancestors to make us sexually different. With wit and a wealth of fascinating examples, he explains how our sexuality has been as crucial as our large brains and upright posture in our rise to human status."},"links":[{"url":"https://www.recordedbooks.com/title-details/9781501995910","title":"recordedbooks.com"},{"url":"http://d2cv0ie6dlin9h.cloudfront.net/C05204/C05204_image_148x230.jpg","title":"RBMedia"}],"isbn_10":["150199591X"],"physical_format":"[sound recording] :","full_title":"Why is sex fun? the evolution of human sexuality","lc_classifications":["HQ21 .D48 2018"],"key":"/books/OL26967338M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL540031A","name":"Jared Diamond"}],"contributions":["Ganser, L. J., narrator","Recorded Books, Inc"],"subjects":["Sex","Sex customs","Physical fitness","HEALTH & FITNESS / Sexuality","Health aspects"],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"pagination":"5 sound discs (5 hr., 15 min.)","source_records":["marc:marc_openlibraries_sanfranciscopubliclibrary/sfpl_chq_2018_12_24_run06.mrc:182514312:3952"],"title":"Why is sex fun?","dewey_decimal_class":["306.7"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"In container (17 cm.).\n\nTitle from container.\n\nCompact disc.\n\nNarrated by L.J. Ganser."},"number_of_pages":15,"isbn_13":["9781501995910"],"edition_name":"Unabridged.","local_id":["urn:sfpl:31223127226208","urn:sfpl:31223127226190","urn:sfpl:31223127226216"],"publish_date":"2018","publish_country":"mdu","by_statement":"Jared Diamond","oclc_numbers":["1043901975"],"works":[{"key":"/works/OL3333181W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"latest_revision":5,"revision":5,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2019-05-24T10:35:54.382980"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2025-06-12T17:19:22.352532"}}},"OLID:OL27095083M":{"bib_key":"OLID:OL27095083M","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL27095083M/Warum_macht_Sex_Spass","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL27095083M/Warum_macht_Sex_Spass","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/8733499-S.jpg","details":{"other_titles":["Why is sex fun? . dt.","330003760."],"publishers":["Bertelsmann"],"subtitle":"die Evolution der menschlichen Sexualität","isbn_10":["3570120082"],"series":["Science Masters"],"covers":[8733499],"key":"/books/OL27095083M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL540031A","name":"Jared Diam